@techReport{Clark1987,
   abstract = {the most economical full-scale GAC operation. Over 10 years ago the U.S. EPA's Drinking Water Research Division initiated a series of field studies to evaluate and characterize the performance of GAC. These field-scale systems consisted of units that are close to modular size for a water treatment plant. The eight utilities who participated in these studies are as follows: Cincinnati, OH; Manchester, NH; Evansville, IN; Jefferson Parish, LA; Miami, FL; Huntington, WV; Beaver Falls, PA; and Pas-saic, NJ (5). Data from these projects are now becoming available for analysis. Information from the two largest projects, Cin-cinnati Water Works and Jefferson Parish, will be analyzed in this paper with an empirical model that was developed for characterizing total organic carbon (TOC) breakthrough curves from granular activated carbon (GAC). The model will be applied to pilot column data from both projects and compared against the results from field-scale equivalents. Data from the Jefferson Parish project will then be applied to scale up projections. A model based on best current cost estimates of "in-place" technology combined with the performance model will be used to estimate a minimum cost design for a full-scale system. Field-Scale Systems The two field-scale systems to which the model will be applied are the Cincinnati Water Works and the Jefferson Parish systems. These two projects, the largest and most extensive of the field-scale projects undertaken by the EPA, are described in the following sections. Cincinnati, Ohio. The primary source water for the Cincinnati Water Works is the Ohio River. Raw water is pumped into large presettling basins having a combined capacity of 1.4 million m3 (372 million gallons) and a retention time of 2-3 days. Alum at an average dose of 17 mg/L is added to the raw water to aid settling. Occasionally , powdered activated carbon (PAC) is added to the raw water as a sunlight screen to deter algae growth in the presettlig basins (taste and odor control) and to lessen the effect of a chemical spill. From the presettling basins, water flows through two parallel water turbine-powered generators that serve as a velocity breaker. The turbine discharge flow is split into two hydraulic jumps, thereby providing rapid mixing of chemicals. Average chemical addition at this point includes lime (17 mg/L), ferric sulfate (8.6 mg/L for high turbidity and 3.4 mg/L for filter conditioning during periods of low turbidity), fluoride (to produce a plant effluent concentration of 0.9 mg/L), and chlorine (typical plant effluent concentration was 1.8 mg/L free of chlorine). The hydraulic jumps direct water to two parallel basins for flocculation followed by clarification. Each basin has a capacity of 49 215 m3 (13 million gallons). Two flumes direct water to 47 rapid sand gravity filters (gradations of gravel and sand), each having an effective area of 130 m2 (1400 ft2) with a hydraulic loading of 6.0 m/h [2.5 gal/min (gpm)/ft2 or 5 million gallons/day (mgd)]. Finished water is directed to either of two underground clear wells before distribution. Total design flow of the plant is 10.3 m3/s (35 mgd) with a typical demand of about 5.7 m3/s (130 mgd). Figure 1 shows the schematic of the Cincinnati treatment works. (1) Modification of Existing Sand Filters. During the GAC study, four of the water treatment plant's 47 sand filters were converted to GAC filters. One other sand filter was used for storage of makeup GAC. Because the Ten State Standards require a minimum of 30.5 cm (12 in.) of filter sand for turbidity removal, only 45.7 cm (18 in.) of one filter's 76.2 cm (30 in.) of sand was removed and reDISTRIBUTION SYSTEM Figure 1. Schematic of Cincinnati, OH, waterworks. placed with Westvaco 12 X 40 WVG GAC (6). With the concurrence of the Ohio EPA, all of the sand (76.2 cm, 30 in.) in three other filters was replaced with GAC. Westvaco 20 X 50 WVW GAC was used in one of these filters to coincide with the efective size (0.45-0.55 mm) of the sand. This filter and the one with 45.7 cm (18 in.) of GAC was used as post sand filtration adsorbers. The other two filters contained 76.2 cm (30 in.) of Westvaco 12 X 40 WVG GAC and were used as filter adsorbers, receiving water before sand filtration (after coagulation/ settling). Average empty bed contact times (EBCT) for all of the modified filters were around 7.5 min. (2) GAC Contactors. The performance of the previously described conventional depth (76.2 cm, 30 in.) gravity filters was compared to deep bed contactors (4.6 m, 5 ft). Four contactor tanks were utilized for this comparison. Each contactor was of cylindrical shell construction of 9.55 mm (3/g in.) thick carbon steel plate designed to the following specifications: diameter vertical sidewall height GAC depth design capacity hydraulic loading contact time design pressure test pressure backwash hydraulic loading 3.4 m (11 ft) 6.7 m (22 ft) 4.6 m (15 ft) 0.04 m3/s (1.0 mgd) 17.8 m/h (7.4 gpm/sft2) 15.3 min 517.1 kPa (75 psig) 620.5 kPa (90 psig) 24 m/h (10 gpm/ft2) The GAC used in each contactor was 12 X 40 WVG. (3) GAC Pilot Columns. Bench-scale glass columns 7.6 cm (3 in.) in diameter were used to simulate the sand replacement filter beds. Two columns 1.4 m (54 in.) in height and containing 76.2 cm (30 in.) of 12 X 40 WVG GAC were utilized. In addition, pilot columns were used for performance comparison to the deep bed contactors. One set of four columns in series contained ICI Hydrodarco 1030 GAC. Each of these columns was 10.2 cm (4 in.) in diameter and 1.8 m (6 ft) in height. The first column in the series had 0.9 m (3 ft) of GAC, and each of three successive column had 1.2 m (4 ft) of GAC for a total GAC depth of 4.6 m (15 ft). Jefferson Parish, Louisiana. The Jefferson Parish study is ongoing and consists of essentially two phases (5). The first portion of the study dealth with conversion of individual sand filters into a filter adsorber and an ad-sorber. The second phase of the operation consists of three contactors each with a design flow of 0.044 m3/s (1 mgd) and an empty bed contact time of 20 min. The regener-ation system is an infrared furnace with a capacity of 97.5},
   author = {Robert M. Clark},
   title = {Evaluating the Cost and Performance of Field-Scale Granular Activated Carbon Systems},
   year = {1987}
}
@techReport{Arnold1986,
   abstract = {Affinity chromatography can be used to measure equilibrium constants and kinetics of biological interactions. The local-equilibrium theory presented in the preceding paper is extended to include mass transfer and kinetic effects. Solutions for both zonal and frontal elution are presented. For highly nonlinear isotherms, the frontal elution method is preferred. Experiments with bovine serum albumin binding to immobilized Reactive Blue show that the binding kinetics inside the porous gel are several orders of magnitude slower than typical biological binding reactions in solution. The temperature dependence of the kinetic constants indicate that the binding may still be diffusion-controlled.},
   author = {F H Arnold and H W Blanch},
   journal = {Journal of Chromatography},
   pages = {13-27},
   title = {ANALYTICAL AFFINITY CHROMATOGRAPHY II. RATE THEORY AND THE MEASUREMENT OF BIOLOGICAL BIND-ING KINETICS},
   volume = {355},
   year = {1986}
}
@article{Hu2020a,
   abstract = {The Clark model was used to describe a fixed-bed adsorption system based on the combination of the mass-transfer concept and the Freundlich isotherm. The previous study revealed a time-dependent mass-transfer coefficient for the heterogeneous diffusion-limited process. Herein, this work proposed the fractal-like Clark model and its reliability was validated by the adsorption of norfloxacin on granular activated carbon. Moreover, the effects of the model parameters on the breakthrough curves and rate profiles were also investigated. The results indicated that the fractal-like Clark model had a high adjusted coefficient of determination (Adj. R2 > 0.977) and a small Akaike information criterion (AIC < −107.2) in all cases, providing more accurate estimation of the model parameters than the Clark model. As a three-parameter model, the Clark model could provide higher fitting accuracy (Adj. R2 = 0.997) for the adsorption of Cu(II) on Cystoseira crinitophylla biomass. The breakthrough curve became more asymmetric when the parameters n and h deviated from 2 and 0, respectively. The fractal-like Clark model may help readers to better understand the mass-transfer process in a fixed-bed column.},
   author = {Qili Hu and Hengyuan Liu and Zhenya Zhang and Xiangjun Pei},
   doi = {10.1016/j.seppur.2020.117396},
   issn = {18733794},
   journal = {Separation and Purification Technology},
   keywords = {Adsorption,Fixed bed,Fractal-like Clark model,Mass-transfer coefficient},
   month = {11},
   publisher = {Elsevier B.V.},
   title = {Development of fractal-like Clark model in a fixed-bed column},
   volume = {251},
   year = {2020}
}
@article{Hu2020b,
   abstract = {In order to more completely describe mathematical characteristics of the breakthrough curve, this work defines the four parameters: Maximum specific breakthrough rate μmax, lag time λ, inflection point ti and half-operating time t50. The breakthrough models include the Bohart–Adams, Thomas, Yoon–Nelson, Clark, Wolborska and dose-response models. Attempts are made to address mathematical relationships between the breakthrough models, propose modified breakthrough models, investigate effects of model parameters on the breakthrough curve and rate profile and reveal their physical meanings. The fitting performance of the breakthrough models is verified by the adsorption of nitrate on the chitosan-Fe(III) composite. The results indicate that the model terms q0m/vc0 and a0x/uc0 are the operating time required to reach 50% breakthrough. The Clark model has the best fitting performance with high adjusted determination factor (Adj. R2 = 0.9976) and low reduced chi-squared value (χ2 = 2.70 × 10−4). In addition, an inconsistency concerning application of the Wolborska model is proposed to avoid this situation where it is repeated in subsequent publications. This work is expected to help readers better understand the breakthrough models and select the appropriate model to analyze the dynamic behaviors in a continuous-flow fixed-bed column.},
   author = {Qili Hu and Yanhua Xie and Zhenya Zhang},
   doi = {10.1016/j.seppur.2019.116399},
   issn = {18733794},
   journal = {Separation and Purification Technology},
   keywords = {Adsorption,Breakthrough model,Modification,Parameters,Rate profile},
   month = {5},
   publisher = {Elsevier B.V.},
   title = {Modification of breakthrough models in a continuous-flow fixed-bed column: Mathematical characteristics of breakthrough curves and rate profiles},
   volume = {238},
   year = {2020}
}
@techReport{Boyer1992,
   abstract = {The effect of &and concentration on both equilibrium and fixed-bed adsorption has been studied for three model proteins on dye-&and adsorbents. Adsorption equilibrium data corresponded well to the Langmuir isotherm. It was found that binding capacity was approximately proportional to immobilized dye concentration. Also it was observed that the more highly substituted adsorbents exhibited higher affinity for proteins. Breakthrough curves were measured for protein adsorption on fixed beds. These data were analyzed by a simple model to determine rate constants for the adsorption process. In general it was found that the rate constants decreased with increasing ligand concentration. The contributions of mass transfer and intrinsic adsorption kinetics to the overall rate were estimated, and it has been found that the adsorption kinetics are rate-limiting. The implications of these results on development of a dye-Iigand process, as well as the validity and utility of this simple model for scale-up aud design procedures, were discussed. Dye-ligand chromatography has become recognized as a powerful technique for the large-scale purification of a wide range of proteins (Scawen and Atkinson, 1987). Several workers have shown that tandem dye-ligand chromatography can be a particularly effective configuration for implementing these ad-sorbents (Hey and Dean, 1983; Scopes, 1984). An important step in the development of such a process is the screening of as many as 75 immobilized dyes in order to identify those which will'be. most suitable for a particular purification task (Scopes, 1986). After selection of candidate dyes, the buffer composition should be adjusted to optimize the interaction of the immobilized dye with the protein. The effects of bufFer composition on protein adsorption have been discussed previously (Bayer and Hsu, 1990). Another factor which is often overlooked in the development of a dye-ligand process is the immobilized dye concentration of the adsorbent. It has been observed that increasing the dye concentration typically increases the biriding capacity of the matrix (Dean and Watson, 1979). However, it has also been shown that proteins adsorbed on the more highly substituted gels are more difficult to elute (Quadri and Dean, 1980). This tighter binding has been attributed to multivalent interactions betwen the protein and the immobilized dye (Liu and Stellwagen, 1986). While this theory does explain many of the observations , there are notable exceptions. Hammond et al. (1986) found that the enzyme bound on either lightly or highly substituted adsorbents was difficult to elute, suggesting that an intermediate range of dye concentration was optimal (Hammond et al., 1986). It is apparent from these observations that ligand cuncen-tration may affect various systems differently, and 'Author to whom correspondence should be addressed. hence should be considered early in the development of the chromatography process. One limitation of these studies is that the information is qualitative and thus does not lend itself to process design. Very little work has been published which shows the elTect of figand concentration on either adsorption equilibrium or kinetics. It is clear that data such as this will be necessary for reliable modeling and scale-up of these adsorption process. In a recent study, equilibrium and kinetic parameters were determined for protein adsorption on two gels containing different amounts of immobilized Cibacron Blue (Herak and Merrill, 1990). However, little discussion of the dye concentration effects was presented. This paper presents the results of a series of experiments in which three model proteins were adsorbed onto Sepharose gels which contained between 1.5 and 25 fimol/crn' Cibacron Blue. Both equilibrium and fixed-bed adsorption were studied. The effects of dye concentration on Langmuir isotherm parameters were determined from the equilibrium data. A simple model, similar to that presented by Chase (1984), was developed to describe the breakthrough curve from a fixed bed of adsorbent subjected to a constant protein feed. Interaction rate constants for protein ad-sorption on the various gels were estimated by comparing experimental breakthrough curves with those generated by the model. The relative rates of the mass transfer processes and the intrinsic adsorption kinetics were also investigated. The implications of these results on the development of a dye-ligand chromatography process and the design of any protein adsorption process are discussed. MODEL DEVELOPMENT The interactions of proteins with immobilized dyes are various and, for the most part, not well understood. The reactive dyes are multifunctional ligands 241},
   author = {Philip M Boyer and James T Hsu'},
   issue = {1},
   journal = {Chemical Engineering Science},
   pages = {241-251},
   title = {EFFECTS OF LIGAND CONCENTRATION ON PROTEIN ADSORPTION IN DYE-LIGAND ADSORBENTS},
   volume = {41},
   year = {1992}
}
@article{Delgado2006,
   abstract = {In this article, a simple and inexpensive experimental technique easy to build in laboratory, for the measurement of tortuosity of a packed bed of inert particles, is described. Experimental values of the tortuosity were obtained with four different packed beds of sand. The experimental results obtained are in good agreement with the theoretical values of tortuosity in a porous media; and the data reported showed the tortuosity to increase with decrease in the void fraction of the packed bed as expected.},
   author = {J. M.P.Q. Delgado},
